From aa3a75eb9da3df80fff6dd39a1ed4276107ffcf1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 07:33:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] A headless wake leaves a turn paused for an update
 to the restarting backend

Chat 20261111_033213_db5894, 2026-11-11 04:10 UTC ("the response disappeared",
desktop-d39268b86c): the Desktop-facing backend paused the chat's turn for a
Hermes update, withdrawing its open clarify form, and restarted. 21 s after the
pause, while the new backend's continuation was still building its agent, the
kanban sweeper of the messaging gateway process woke the chat for a waiting
notice. _wake_session_turn cold-resumed it there; session.resume scheduled the
paused turn's continuation in the gateway process (the notice itself was
refused "busy" and written as a row). No Desktop window is attached to that
process and open server requests are per process, so the re-asked form was
never shown; the Desktop backend logged "session has another live owner", and
the user's next message took the chat over and cancelled the form.

_wake_session_turn now answers "busy" without resuming while the unloaded chat
carries a core-update pause whose writer is gone and that is younger than
SUCCESSOR_YIELD_S (= KICKOFF_HOLD_S, the bound the continuation kickoff already
has). Callers treat it as today: the sweeper writes the passive notice row, the
automation replay rechecks. Past the window, or with a live writer, another
marker reason, or auto_continue off, nothing changes: any reader continues.

RED on fd8e708c411: test_headless_wake_leaves_a_fresh_paused_turn_to_its_successor
fails "the wake took the paused turn's continuation". GREEN: 9 files, 110 passed.
